{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01377 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Magnesium Oxide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving phosphorus metabolism. The evidence suggests that Magnesium Oxide may influence phosphorus homeostasis, which in turn affects calcium levels. Specifically, Magnesium Oxide is linked to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, a condition that is associated with Calcium Acetate or Calcium Glucoheptonate, both of which are known to contribute to hypercalcemia. These findings collectively imply that Magnesium Oxide may modulate calcium metabolism by affecting phosphorus levels, potentially leading to elevated calcium concentrations in the blood. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Magnesium Oxide could contribute to the development of Hypercalcemia Disease, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
